/service-areas rendered all 100 published areas as a single grid. On a phone
that is a hundred cards in one column before the page says anything about
what is done in them or what it costs, and for an assistant it is a hundred
sibling links with no structure to summarise. It is the one genuine
scrolling marathon on this site — the glossary is already grouped by letter
with a jump nav, and the footer already uses native accordions on mobile.

THE GROUPING IS THE DATA'S OWN SHAPE

content/geo/corridors.ts already models eleven routes, curated by somebody
who drives them, and `corridorsFor` already resolves a district through its
municipality. Measured before a line was written: all 100 published areas map
to a corridor and none falls outside one. The distribution is real —
core-gta 40, buffalo-niagara 11, niagara-belt 9, rochester-east 8, and so on
down to two corridors with three each.

That measurement mattered. A grouping that put ninety cities in one bucket
would have rendered fine and still been a marathon, so the test asserts the
largest group holds under three quarters of the total rather than trusting
today's numbers to hold.

WHAT IT COSTS A CRAWLER: NOTHING

Native <details>, no JavaScript, no useState — the same mechanism the footer
already uses. Collapsed <details> content is still parsed and still crawled,
so all one hundred links are in the document whether a group is open or shut.
A JavaScript-gated list would not be. The largest group is open by default so
the page never looks empty, and a jump nav lets a human and a machine skip
straight to a corridor.

ONE CITY, ONE GROUP

A market can sit on two corridors — that is what a corridor is. Here each
city is listed under the first, once. Listing it under both would turn a
hundred links into more than a hundred, make every count in every summary
wrong, and hand a crawler duplicate paths to one page from one document. The
corridor pages are where full membership belongs and they already show it.

`cottage-north-east` is a real route with no published area on it. It renders
as nothing rather than as a group with a zero beside it: an empty accordion
is a promise of content that is not there.

A city on NO corridor would land in a trailing "Elsewhere" group rather than
being dropped. There are none today; the branch exists so that adding a
market without corridor membership makes it appear somewhere instead of
vanishing from the index in silence.

TWO GUARDS AND A COUNT

The test that earns its place is the count: a grouped index that drops or
duplicates a city is worse than the flat grid it replaced, and neither
failure is visible by looking at the page.

parse-scan caught a `${` inside a plain string in the test — the shape that
is almost always a template literal typed with the wrong quotes. Split rather
than excepted.

And an assertion that the page contains no "hidden" failed on `aria-hidden`
in the jump nav I had just written. That is the fourth word-shaped needle
this session to match prose instead of code. It now checks the two things
that would actually remove a link from the document: a render gated on open
state, and a display:none.

Verified: 65 of 66 guards. verify:css needs postcss from node_modules, absent
here. verify:links unchanged — 46 routes, 0 orphans. parse-scan 620 files,
zero diagnostics. Resolving typecheck clean. 64 assertions.

